# A warning about `filter()`: check for `NA` {visibility="hidden"}

## Warning on `filter()`: it can silently drop your `NA`s

- Suppose we want to remove the `"Poor"` category from `srh` (self-reported health)

::: columns
::: column

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
hrs_00 |> tabyl(srh)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
       srh   n     percent valid_percent
 Excellent 277 0.101539589    0.10168869
 Very Good 647 0.237170088    0.23751836
      Good 870 0.318914956    0.31938326
      Fair 743 0.272360704    0.27276065
      Poor 187 0.068548387    0.06864905
      <NA>   4 0.001466276            NA
```


:::
:::

:::
::: column


`srh` has 4 missing values!
:::
:::

::: columns
::: column
When we try to filter using `!=`, we **lose our `NA`s**!


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
hrs_03_rm_poor <- hrs_00 |> 
  filter(srh != "Poor")
```
:::

:::
::: column
When `R` checks `NA != "Poor"`, it evaluates to `NA`, not `TRUE`, so that row gets dropped


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
hrs_03_rm_poor |> tabyl(srh)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
       srh   n   percent
 Excellent 277 0.1091841
 Very Good 647 0.2550256
      Good 870 0.3429247
      Fair 743 0.2928656
      Poor   0 0.0000000
```


:::
:::

:::
:::

## Use `filter_out()` to keep `NA`s

::: columns
::: {.column width="60%"}

- We can use `filter_out()` from the `dplyr` package to keep the `NA`s when we want to perform an exclusionary (`!=`) filter


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
hrs_04_rm_poor <- hrs_00 |>
  filter_out(srh == "Poor")

hrs_04_rm_poor |> tabyl(srh)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
       srh   n     percent valid_percent
 Excellent 277 0.109012200     0.1091841
 Very Good 647 0.254624164     0.2550256
      Good 870 0.342384888     0.3429247
      Fair 743 0.292404565     0.2928656
      Poor   0 0.000000000     0.0000000
      <NA>   4 0.001574183            NA
```


:::
:::


:::
::: {.column width="40%"}



::: pink
::: pink-ttl
Build this habit
:::
::: pink-cont
Whenever you filter on a column that has missing values, stop and check: *how many observations were removed and does that correspond to the number of values I expect (excluding `NA`s?)*
:::
:::
